nal symptoms (fever, weight loss) provide stronger evidence for lymphoid neoplasm (B2) than for a blast crisis in chronic myeloid neoplasm (B3)?", "summary": "35% blasts in peripheral blood is a hallmark of acute lymphoid neoplasms (B2) with LR+ 6.0. Constitutional symptoms align better with lymphoid processes, while blast crisis of CML (B3) typically presents with myeloid blast lineage, making this a moderate_for for B2 and strong_against for B3."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the subacute constitutional syndrome and 35% blasts represent CML in blast crisis, given the absence of chronic CML-specific features like splenomegaly?", "summary": "The absence of splenomegaly (a common feature in chronic myeloid neoplasms) and the lymphoid blast morphology (implied by '35% blasts' in context) strongly argue against CML in blast crisis (B3), which typically shows myeloid blasts. This is strong_against for B3 and neutral for B2."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the acute visual/ataxic findings better explained by a myeloid process (B3) or do they contradict lymphoid neoplasm (B2) as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Acute neurological/visual symptoms are more commonly associated with systemic inflammation or CNS involvement in lymphoid processes (B2), whereas myeloid neoplasms (B3) typically lack such features. This provides moderate_for for B2 and weak_against for B3."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of splenomegaly on physical exam significantly undermine the likelihood of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B3) as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Splenomegaly is a common finding in chronic myeloproliferative disorders (B3). Its absence on physical exam provides strong_against evidence for B3 and neutral impact for B2."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.486], ["B4", 0.193], ["B2", 0.119]]}, {"top": [["B3", 0.495], ["B2", 0.293], ["B4", 0.141]]}], "deliberation": {}, "i